2. Related Theoretical Work

2.1 Geometric Deep Learning

Geometric Deep Learning provides a framework for incorporating symmetries and manifold structures into neural network architectures. The MGWU extends this philosophy by treating the representation spaces themselves as manifolds with domain‑specific metrics, rather than merely imposing equivariance constraints on a single Euclidean space.

2.2 World Models and JEPA

The Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) learns predictive representations in a single latent space. While successful, JEPA does not explicitly separate distinct mathematical structures. MGWU argues that a single space cannot simultaneously represent continuous Lie group symmetries, discrete directed graphs, and hyperbolic hierarchies without metric distortion, and proposes explicit separation as a remedy.

2.3 Causal Representation Learning

Causal representation learning (CRL) aims to recover latent causal variables and their relationships from high‑dimensional observations. Recent theoretical results establish identifiability of latent causal graphs under interventional data. MGWU incorporates CRL by defining a causal geometry whose metric is a differentiable shortest‑path distance on a learned DAG; we now further extend this to a Finsler geometry that naturally encodes asymmetry.

2.4 Modularity and Multi‑Expert Architectures

Modularity has been explored in cognitive science and machine learning, but typically within a single shared representation space. MGWU differs by assigning each module its own manifold with distinct metric properties, aligning them only through a low‑dimensional core.

2.5 Port‑Hamiltonian Systems and Passivity‑Based Control

Port‑Hamiltonian theory provides a geometric framework for modelling physical systems with energy exchange, dissipation, and control. It has been applied in robotics and neural network design to ensure stability. We adopt this formalism to couple the physical geometry with the dynamical core, enabling principled integration of external actuation and ensuring energy consistency.

