5. Cross‑Geometric Alignment and the Dynamical Core

For each geometry G_i, we define an encoder e_i: \mathcal{M}_i \to \mathcal{W}_{\text{dyn}} and a decoder d_i: \mathcal{W}_{\text{dyn}} \to \mathcal{M}_i. The core state z_{\text{core}} \in \mathcal{W}_{\text{dyn}} evolves according to a learned transition model

z_{\text{core}}(t+1) = f_\theta(z_{\text{core}}(t), a_t) + \epsilon,

where a_t is an action and \epsilon is process noise. In the next sections, we will replace this generic transition with a Hamiltonian or port‑Hamiltonian evolution for the coupled physical–core system.

5.1 Scale‑Normalised Star‑Topology Consistency

Given a batch of N paired observations, each geometry i and the core produce a set of representations. We compute pairwise distance matrices D_i and D_{\text{core}}. To account for different scales, we use mean‑normalised matrices:

\tilde{D}_i[a,b] = \frac{D_i[a,b]}{\frac{1}{N^2}\sum_{u,v} D_i[u,v] + \epsilon}.

The cross‑geometric consistency loss is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{CGC}} = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} \lambda_i \lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_{\text{core}} \rVert_F^2.

This star topology reduces computational cost from \mathcal{O}(K^2 N^2) to \mathcal{O}(K N^2), and transitivity ensures pairwise alignment: if each geometry aligns with the core, then \lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_j \rVert_F \le \delta_i + \delta_j.

Rank distortion consideration. Euclidean core distance matrices have rank at most d_{\text{core}} + 2, whereas hyperbolic or graph‑based distances may have higher rank. To mitigate forced distortion, we augment \mathcal{L}_{\text{CGC}} with a local neighbourhood preservation loss that aligns only the k-nearest neighbour graphs:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{local}} = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} \sum_{a,b} w_{ab}^{(i)} \left( \mathbf{1}[b \in \mathcal{N}_k(a; G_i)] - \mathbf{1}[b \in \mathcal{N}_k(a; \text{core})] \right)^2,

where \mathcal{N}_k(a; \cdot) denotes the set of k-nearest neighbours of a. The total alignment loss is \mathcal{L}_{\text{align}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{CGC}} + \lambda_{\text{local}} \mathcal{L}_{\text{local}}.

5.2 Lipschitz Bounds for Projections

Projections between non‑Euclidean manifolds and the Euclidean core are subject to metric distortion. We recall the previously derived bounds.

Hyperbolic to Euclidean. For the encoder e_L: \mathbb{H}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d, the Riemannian operator norm of its differential is

\lVert D e_L(x) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathbb{H} \to \mathbb{R}} = \frac{1 - \lVert x \rVert^2}{2} \lVert J_x \rVert_{\text{op}},

so the global Lipschitz constant K_L \le \frac{1}{2} \sup_x \lVert J_x \rVert_{\text{op}}. The map is contracting near the boundary. For the decoder d_L: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{H}^d,

\lVert D d_L(z) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{H}} = \frac{2}{1 - \lVert d_L(z) \rVert^2} \lVert J_z^{dec} \rVert_{\text{op}},

which diverges as d_L(z) approaches the boundary. We therefore constrain the decoder output norm to \lVert d_L(z) \rVert \le 1 - \epsilon.

\mathrm{SE}(3) to Euclidean. For the encoder e_P: \mathrm{SE}(3) \to \mathbb{R}^d,

\lVert D e_P(g) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathrm{SE}(3) \to \mathbb{R}} \le \frac{\lVert J_g^{\mathfrak{se}(3)} \rVert_{\text{op}}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{W})}},

where \sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{W}) is the smallest eigenvalue of the mass‑inertia matrix. As long as all objects have strictly positive mass and inertia, this bound is global and finite.

5.3 Collapse Prevention via VICReg

To prevent representational collapse, we apply VICReg regularisation to each geometry and the core. For a batch Z \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d}, the variance loss is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{var}}(Z) = \frac{1}{d} \sum_{k=1}^{d} \max\left(0, \gamma - \sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(Z[:,k])}\right),

and the covariance loss is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{cov}}(Z) = \frac{1}{d^2} \sum_{k \neq l} \operatorname{Cov}(Z[:,k], Z[:,l])^2.

The total regularisation is \mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}} = \sum_{i \in \{\text{geometries}, \text{core}\}} \left( \mathcal{L}_{\text{var}}^{(i)} + \mathcal{L}_{\text{cov}}^{(i)} \right). If \mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}} = 0, then every feature has variance at least \gamma and features are pairwise uncorrelated, which provably prevents collapse to a constant representation.

