6. Finsler Causal Geometry

To rigorously model asymmetric reachability, the causal geometry G_C is extended from a Riemannian manifold (\mathcal{M}_C, g_C) to a Finsler manifold (\mathcal{M}_C, F_C), where F_C: T\mathcal{M}_C \to [0, \infty) is a continuous, asymmetric Minkowski norm defined on the tangent bundle.

Definition 1 (Finsler Causal Structure). A Finsler structure F_C(x, v) on the smooth manifold \mathcal{M}_C satisfies:

· Smoothness: F_C(x, v) is C^\infty on T\mathcal{M}_C \setminus \{0\}.
· Positive Homogeneity: F_C(x, \lambda v) = \lambda F_C(x, v) for all \lambda > 0.
· Strong Convexity: The fundamental tensor g_{ij}(x, v) = \frac{1}{2} \frac{\partial^2 F_C^2(x, v)}{\partial v^i \partial v^j} is positive definite for all v \neq 0.
· Directional Asymmetry (Non‑Reversibility): In general, F_C(x, -v) \neq F_C(x, v), which explicitly breaks time‑reversal/directional symmetry in tangent space.

6.1 Causal Randers Structure & Drift Mechanics

A natural specialization for modeling directed transport and influence flow is the Randers metric, a perturbed Riemannian metric incorporating a directional vector/1‑form field.

Definition 2 (Causal Randers Metric). The Finsler structure F_C(x, v) is parameterized as:

F_C(x, v) = \alpha(x, v) + \beta(x, v) = \sqrt{a_{ij}(x) v^i v^j} + b_i(x) v^i,

where:

· a_{ij}(x) dx^i dx^j is a symmetric Riemannian metric encoding ambient local graph density.
· b(x) = b_i(x) dx^i is a learned 1‑form representing the causal drift field.

To guarantee strong convexity, the anisotropic drift norm is constrained such that \lVert b \rVert_a = \sqrt{a^{ij}(x) b_i(x) b_j(x)} < 1.

Interpretation of Causal Drift. The vector b^i(x) = a^{ij}(x) b_j(x) points in the direction of downstream causal flow. Moving along the causal arrow (v aligned with b) decreases the tangent cost F_C(x, v), whereas moving against the causal arrow (v anti‑aligned with b) increases F_C(x, v):

\begin{array}{ccc}
\text{Downstream } (v \sim +b) & & \text{Upstream } (-v \sim -b) \\
F_C(x,v) = \lVert v \rVert_a + \langle b, v \rangle & \qquad & F_C(x,-v) = \lVert v \rVert_a - \langle b, v \rangle \\
\text{Low asymmetric distance} & & \text{High asymmetric distance}
\end{array}

6.2 Asymmetric Geodesic Distance and Causal Cones

Given a piecewise smooth path \gamma: [0, 1] \to \mathcal{M}_C with \gamma(0) = p and \gamma(1) = q, the asymmetric Finsler arc length is:

L_{F_C}(\gamma) = \int_0^1 F_C\left(\gamma(t), \dot{\gamma}(t)\right) dt.

Definition 3 (Finsler Quasi‑Metric). The causal asymmetric distance d_F(p, q) from point p to point q is defined as:

d_F(p, q) = \inf_{\gamma \in \mathcal{P}(p, q)} L_{F_C}(\gamma),

where \mathcal{P}(p, q) is the set of all oriented paths from p to q. Crucially:

d_F(p, q) \neq d_F(q, p) \quad \text{for } p \neq q.

Causal Cones. The forward causal cone C_p^+ at p \in \mathcal{M}_C for a maximum reachability threshold R > 0 is defined by:

C_p^+ = \{ q \in \mathcal{M}_C : d_F(p, q) \le R \}.

Because F_C(x, v) < F_C(x, -v) along the drift b, C_p^+ stretches preferentially downstream along the directed edges of the learned DAG.

6.3 Continuous Graph Mapping & Differentiable Eikonal Solver

To bridge the Finsler metric F_C(x, v) with the discrete adjacency matrix A and edge lengths W from Section 4.4, we construct a_{ij}(x) and b_i(x) via node embeddings z_i \in \mathcal{M}_C.

Metric Formulation: a_{ij}(x) = \delta_{ij} \cdot \psi_\theta(x), where \psi_\theta(x) > 0 is a scalar field parameterized by a multi‑layer perceptron.

Causal Drift Field Formulation: b(x) = \tanh\left( \text{MLP}_\phi(x) \right) \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \epsilon}, ensuring \lVert b(x) \rVert_a < 1 globally.

Asymmetric Distance Computation via Non‑symmetric Eikonal Equation: The forward distance map u(x) = d_F(p, x) from source point p is the unique viscosity solution to the dual Finsler–Eikonal equation:

F_C^*\left(x, d_x u\right) = 1, \quad u(p) = 0,

where F_C^*(x, y) is the dual Finsler metric on T^*\mathcal{M}_C:

F_C^*(x, y) = \sup \{ y_i v^i : v \in T_x \mathcal{M}_C, \, F_C(x, v) \le 1 \}.

For the Randers metric, the dual norm simplifies to:

F_C^*(x, y) = \frac{\sqrt{\left(a^{ij} y_i y_j\right)\left(1 - \lVert b \rVert_a^2\right) + \left(a^{ij} y_i b_j\right)^2} - a^{ij} y_i b_j}{1 - \lVert b \rVert_a^2}.

This Hamilton–Jacobi formulation allows for differentiable, neural‑network‑compatible solvers (e.g., Anisotropic Fast Marching or Neural PDE Solvers) to compute d_F(p, q) across all latent nodes in a single forward pass.

6.4 Theoretical Properties of the Finsler Causal Distance

Proposition 7 (Asymmetry Bound under Randers Drift). Let \gamma_{p \to q} be the minimizing forward geodesic from p to q under F_C, and \bar{\gamma}_{q \to p} be the time‑reversed curve. The degree of directional asymmetry is bounded by the circulation of the drift 1‑form b:

d_F(q, p) - d_F(p, q) \ge -2 \int_{\gamma_{p \to q}} b_i(x) dx^i.

Proof. By definition of the Randers metric:

d_F(p, q) = \int_0^1 \left( \alpha(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) + \beta(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) \right) dt.

For the reversed path \bar{\gamma}(t) = \gamma(1-t), we have \dot{\bar{\gamma}}(t) = -\dot{\gamma}(1-t). Since \alpha(x, -v) = \alpha(x, v) and \beta(x, -v) = -\beta(x, v):

L_{F_C}(\bar{\gamma}) = \int_0^1 \left( \alpha(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) - \beta(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) \right) dt.

Because d_F(q, p) \le L_{F_C}(\bar{\gamma}), taking the difference gives:

d_F(q, p) - d_F(p, q) \ge \int_0^1 \left( \alpha - \beta \right) dt - \int_0^1 \left( \alpha + \beta \right) dt = -2 \int_0^1 b_i(\gamma(t)) \dot{\gamma}^i(t) dt = -2 \int_{\gamma_{p \to q}} b_i(x) dx^i.

∎

Theorem 8 (Quasi‑Metric Properties of d_F). The asymmetric distance function d_F: \mathcal{M}_C \times \mathcal{M}_C \to [0, \infty) satisfies all quasi‑metric axioms:

1. Positivity: d_F(p, q) \ge 0, and d_F(p, q) = 0 \iff p = q.
2. Directed Triangle Inequality: For any p, q, r \in \mathcal{M}_C:

d_F(p, r) \le d_F(p, q) + d_F(q, r).

Proof. Positivity follows directly from strong convexity and positive definiteness of F_C(x, v) for v \neq 0 since \lVert b \rVert_a < 1.

Let \gamma_1: [0, 1] \to \mathcal{M}_C be the minimizing geodesic from p to q, and \gamma_2: [0, 1] \to \mathcal{M}_C from q to r. Concatenate \gamma = \gamma_1 * \gamma_2 on [0, 2]. By reparameterization invariance (homogeneity of degree 1):

d_F(p, r) \le L_{F_C}(\gamma) = L_{F_C}(\gamma_1) + L_{F_C}(\gamma_2) = d_F(p, q) + d_F(q, r).

∎

