8. Port‑Hamiltonian Extension for Open Systems

To model open physical interactions, dissipation (e.g., joint friction, air resistance), and exogenous actuation inputs, the closed Lie–Poisson system on \mathcal{M}_{\text{ext}} is extended to an Implicit Port‑Hamiltonian (pH) System on a Dirac structure.

8.1 Extended Port‑Hamiltonian State Space

The extended state vector remains x = (q, \mu, z) \in \mathcal{M}_{\text{ext}}. We introduce three explicit energy port channels:

· Internal Dissipation Port: (\mathcal{F}_R, \mathcal{E}_R) where flows \mathcal{F}_R and efforts \mathcal{E}_R satisfy resistive constitutive relations.
· Physical Control Port: (u_P, y_P) \in \mathfrak{se}(3)^K \times (\mathfrak{se}(3)^*)^K representing external forces/wrenches (inputs u_P) and co‑located generalized body velocities (outputs y_P).
· Core Control/Drive Port: (u_C, y_C) \in \mathbb{R}^{m} \times \mathbb{R}^{m} representing exogenous latent interventions/drive signals.

8.2 Dirac Structure and Interconnection Operator

The system power flow is defined by the skew‑symmetric interconnection tensor \mathcal{J}(x) and the positive semi‑definite dissipation tensor \mathcal{R}(x) \succeq 0:

\mathcal{J}(x) = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & -L_g & 0 \\
L_g^* & -\operatorname{ad}^*_{\square}\mu & 0 \\
0 & 0 & J(z)
\end{pmatrix},
\quad
\mathcal{R}(x) = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \mathbf{D}_P(q, \mu) & 0 \\
0 & 0 & \mathbf{D}_C(z)
\end{pmatrix},

where:

· \mathbf{D}_P(q, \mu) \succeq 0 is the physical damping matrix mapping Lie algebra velocities to dissipative co‑vectors in \mathfrak{se}(3)^*.
· \mathbf{D}_C(z) \succeq 0 is the core latent dissipation matrix preventing unbounded latent oscillations.

Power‑Conserving Port Interconnection. The explicit Port‑Hamiltonian state‑space representation is:

\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{q} \\ \dot{\mu} \\ \dot{z}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\Big( \mathcal{J}(x) - \mathcal{R}(x) \Big) \nabla H_{\text{total}}(x) + \mathbf{g}(x) u,
\qquad
y = \mathbf{g}^T(x) \nabla H_{\text{total}}(x),

where the input‑to‑state mapping matrix \mathbf{g}(x) and control vector u are structured as:

\mathbf{g}(x) = \begin{bmatrix}
0 & 0 \\
\mathbf{B}_P(q) & 0 \\
0 & \mathbf{B}_C(z)
\end{bmatrix},
\quad
u = \begin{bmatrix} u_P \\ u_C \end{bmatrix},
\quad
y = \begin{bmatrix} y_P \\ y_C \end{bmatrix}.

8.3 Explicit Equations of Motion

Evaluating \left( \mathcal{J}(x) - \mathcal{R}(x) \right) \nabla H_{\text{total}}(x) + \mathbf{g}(x) u component‑wise yields the Port‑Hamiltonian extensions to the Lie–Poisson dynamics.

1. Kinematic Flow on \mathrm{SE}(3)^K:

\dot{g}_k = g_k \cdot (\xi_k^{\text{eff}})^\wedge,

where the effective velocity twist \xi_k^{\text{eff}} \in \mathfrak{se}(3) remains:

\xi_k^{\text{eff}} = \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k} = \mathbf{W}_k^{-1} \mu_k + \lambda \left( D_{\mu_k} e_P \right)^* (e_P(q, \mu) - z).

2. Damped and Actuated Momentum Flow on \mathfrak{se}(3)^*:

\dot{\mu}_k = \operatorname{ad}^*_{\xi_k^{\text{eff}}} \mu_k - L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial V}{\partial g_k} - \tau_k^{\text{core}} - \underbrace{\mathbf{D}_{P,k}(q_k) \, \xi_k^{\text{eff}}}_{\text{Physical Dissipation Wrench}} + \underbrace{\mathbf{B}_{P,k}(q_k) \, u_{P,k}}_{\text{External Actuation Wrench}},

where:

· \tau_k^{\text{core}} = \lambda L_{g_k}^* \left( D_{g_k} e_P \right)^* (e_P(q, \mu) - z) is the core feedback wrench.
· \mathbf{D}_{P,k} \succeq 0 governs physical drag/friction forces.
· \mathbf{B}_{P,k}(q_k) maps physical control wrenches u_{P,k} into the body frame.

3. Core Latent State Dynamics with Dissipation and Drive:

\dot{z} = \Big( J(z) - \mathbf{D}_C(z) \Big) \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} + \mathbf{B}_C(z) u_C.

Expanding \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} = \nabla_z H_{\text{core}}(z) - \lambda (e_P(q, \mu) - z):

\dot{z} = \Big( J(z) - \mathbf{D}_C(z) \Big) \left( \nabla_z H_{\text{core}}(z) - \lambda (e_P(q, \mu) - z) \right) + \mathbf{B}_C(z) u_C.

4. Co‑located System Output Equations. The conjugated power outputs y = [y_P; y_C] are:

y_{P, k} = \mathbf{B}_{P,k}^T(q_k) \, \xi_k^{\text{eff}} \in \mathfrak{se}(3)^*, \quad k \in \{1, \dots, K\},

y_C = \mathbf{B}_C^T(z) \left( \nabla_z H_{\text{core}}(z) - \lambda (e_P(q, \mu) - z) \right) \in \mathbb{R}^m.

8.4 Power Balance and Dissipation Theorem

Theorem 10 (Port‑Hamiltonian Energy Power Balance). For any trajectories x(t) = (q(t), \mu(t), z(t)) satisfying the Port‑Hamiltonian Lie–Poisson equations of motion, the rate of change of total system energy satisfies the strict dissipation inequality:

\frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}}(x(t)) = -\mathcal{P}_{\text{diss}}(t) + \mathcal{P}_{\text{ext}}(t) \le \mathcal{P}_{\text{ext}}(t),

where:

· Dissipated Power: \mathcal{P}_{\text{diss}} = \sum_{k=1}^K \left( \xi_k^{\text{eff}} \right)^T \mathbf{D}_{P,k} \, \xi_k^{\text{eff}} + \left( \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} \right)^T \mathbf{D}_C(z) \, \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} \ge 0
· External Supplied Power: \mathcal{P}_{\text{ext}} = y_P^T u_P + y_C^T u_C = \langle y, u \rangle.

Proof. Using the total time derivative \frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}} = \left( \nabla H_{\text{total}} \right)^T \dot{x}:

\frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}} = \left( \nabla H_{\text{total}} \right)^T \left[ \Big( \mathcal{J}(x) - \mathcal{R}(x) \Big) \nabla H_{\text{total}} + \mathbf{g}(x) u \right].

Conservative Interconnection Term: By skew‑symmetry of \mathcal{J}(x) = -\mathcal{J}^T(x):

\left( \nabla H_{\text{total}} \right)^T \mathcal{J}(x) \nabla H_{\text{total}} = 0.

Dissipative Term: Since \mathcal{R}(x) = \operatorname{blockdiag}(0, \mathbf{D}_P, \mathbf{D}_C) \succeq 0:

-\left( \nabla H_{\text{total}} \right)^T \mathcal{R}(x) \nabla H_{\text{total}} = -\sum_{k=1}^K \left( \xi_k^{\text{eff}} \right)^T \mathbf{D}_{P,k} \, \xi_k^{\text{eff}} - \left( \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} \right)^T \mathbf{D}_C(z) \, \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} = -\mathcal{P}_{\text{diss}} \le 0.

External Power Input Term: Using the output definition y = \mathbf{g}^T(x) \nabla H_{\text{total}}:

\left( \nabla H_{\text{total}} \right)^T \mathbf{g}(x) u = \left( \mathbf{g}^T(x) \nabla H_{\text{total}} \right)^T u = y^T u = \mathcal{P}_{\text{ext}}.

Combining all three terms yields:

\frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}} = -\mathcal{P}_{\text{diss}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{ext}}.

Since \mathcal{P}_{\text{diss}} \ge 0, we establish \frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}} \le y^T u, proving that the system is passivity‑preserving with respect to the input‑output pair (u, y) and storage function H_{\text{total}}. ∎

8.5 Architectural Summary & Control Implications

The Port‑Hamiltonian structure provides two major theoretical capabilities:

· Passivity‑Based Control (PBC): Control laws u_P(y_P) or u_C(y_C) can be designed via Interconnection and Damping Assignment (IDA‑PBC) to asymptotically stabilize target physical poses or latent goal states without risking un‑modeled energy growth.
· Robust Multi‑Geometry Coupling under Noise: Dissipation matrices \mathbf{D}_P, \mathbf{D}_C act as natural geometric filters, absorbing high‑frequency discretization artifacts or prediction noise introduced during gradient updates of the dynamical core.

