Appendix B: Cross‑Geometric Alignment and Lipschitz Bounds

B.1 Scale‑Normalized Star‑Topology Consistency Loss

For a batch of N paired observations, each geometry i \in \mathcal{G} and the dynamical core produce a set of representations. We compute the raw pairwise distance matrix D_i for each geometry and D_{\text{core}} for the core. To handle differing scales, we use mean‑normalized distance matrices:

\tilde{D}_i[a,b] = \frac{D_i[a,b]}{\frac{1}{N^2}\sum_{u=1}^N \sum_{v=1}^N D_i[u,v] + \epsilon},

where \epsilon > 0 is a small constant for numerical stability. The cross‑geometric consistency loss is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{CGC}} = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} \lambda_i \left\lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_{\text{core}} \right\rVert_F^2,

with scalar weights \lambda_i > 0. This star topology reduces complexity from \mathcal{O}(K^2 N^2) to \mathcal{O}(K N^2).

Proposition 1 (Scale invariance).
The loss \mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}^{(i,j)} = \lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_j \rVert_F^2 is invariant to scaling of the raw distance matrices by positive constants.

Proof.
Let D_i' = c_i D_i and D_j' = c_j D_j with c_i, c_j > 0. Then \mu_i' = c_i \mu_i and

\tilde{D}_i' = \frac{c_i D_i}{c_i \mu_i + \epsilon}.

For \epsilon \ll \mu_i, \tilde{D}_i' \approx \frac{D_i}{\mu_i} = \tilde{D}_i. The approximation becomes exact as \epsilon \to 0. Similarly for j. Thus the loss is invariant up to numerical precision. ∎

Proposition 2 (Transitivity of star‑topology alignment).
If \lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_{\text{core}} \rVert_F \le \delta_i for all i, then for any pair i,j, \lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_j \rVert_F \le \delta_i + \delta_j.

Proof.
By the triangle inequality for the Frobenius norm,

\lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_j \rVert_F = \lVert (\tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_{\text{core}}) + (\tilde{D}_{\text{core}} - \tilde{D}_j) \rVert_F \le \lVert \tilde{D}_i - \tilde{D}_{\text{core}} \rVert_F + \lVert \tilde{D}_{\text{core}} - \tilde{D}_j \rVert_F \le \delta_i + \delta_j.

∎

B.2 Local Neighborhood Preservation Loss

Because a Euclidean core of low dimension cannot exactly match the full distance matrices of non‑Euclidean geometries, we augment \mathcal{L}_{\text{CGC}} with a local neighborhood preservation term. For each geometry i and the core, let \mathcal{N}_k(a; G_i) denote the set of indices of the k nearest neighbors of point a in geometry i, and similarly \mathcal{N}_k(a; \text{core}). The local loss is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{local}} = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} \sum_{a=1}^N \sum_{b \in \mathcal{N}_k(a; G_i) \cup \mathcal{N}_k(a; \text{core})} \left( \mathbf{1}[b \in \mathcal{N}_k(a; G_i)] - \mathbf{1}[b \in \mathcal{N}_k(a; \text{core})] \right)^2,

where \mathbf{1}[\cdot] is the indicator function. In practice, this can be implemented with a differentiable relaxation (e.g., using softmax over distances) or as a ranking loss. The total alignment loss is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{CGC}} + \lambda_{\text{local}} \mathcal{L}_{\text{local}}.

B.3 Lipschitz Bounds for Projections

Projections between non‑Euclidean manifolds and the Euclidean core are subject to metric distortion. We derive explicit bounds to ensure stable gradient flow.

B.3.1 Hyperbolic to Euclidean

For the encoder e_L: \mathbb{H}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d, let J_x \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times n} be the Euclidean Jacobian of e_L at point x \in \mathbb{B}^n. The Riemannian operator norm of the differential D e_L(x): T_x \mathbb{B}^n \to T_{e_L(x)} \mathbb{R}^d is

\lVert D e_L(x) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathbb{H} \to \mathbb{R}} = \sup_{v \neq 0} \frac{\lVert J_x v \rVert_{\mathbb{R}^d}}{\lambda_x \lVert v \rVert_{\mathbb{E}}} = \frac{\lVert J_x \rVert_{\text{op}}}{\lambda_x},

where \lambda_x = \frac{2}{1 - \lVert x \rVert^2} is the conformal factor. Thus

\lVert D e_L(x) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathbb{H} \to \mathbb{R}} = \frac{1 - \lVert x \rVert^2}{2} \lVert J_x \rVert_{\text{op}}.

Let J_{\max} = \sup_{x \in \mathbb{B}^n} \lVert J_x \rVert_{\text{op}}. Then the global Lipschitz constant K_L satisfies

K_L \le \frac{J_{\max}}{2}.

The map is naturally contracting near the boundary (\lVert x \rVert \to 1).

For the decoder d_L: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{H}^d, let J_z^{dec} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d} be the Euclidean Jacobian at core state z. The operator norm mapping Euclidean tangent vectors into hyperbolic space is

\lVert D d_L(z) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{H}} = \sup_{w \neq 0} \frac{\lVert J_z^{dec} w \rVert_{d_L(z)}}{\lVert w \rVert_{\mathbb{R}^d}} = \lambda_{d_L(z)} \lVert J_z^{dec} \rVert_{\text{op}} = \frac{2}{1 - \lVert d_L(z) \rVert^2} \lVert J_z^{dec} \rVert_{\text{op}}.

As d_L(z) approaches the boundary, this norm diverges. To prevent gradient explosion, we constrain the decoder output norm to \lVert d_L(z) \rVert \le 1 - \epsilon for some small \epsilon > 0.

B.3.2 \mathrm{SE}(3) to Euclidean

For the encoder e_P: \mathrm{SE}(3) \to \mathbb{R}^d, let J_g^{\mathfrak{se}(3)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times 6} be the Jacobian of e_P with respect to the left‑invariant frame fields. The operator norm with respect to the mass‑inertia weighted metric \mathbf{W} is

\lVert D e_P(g) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathrm{SE}(3) \to \mathbb{R}} = \sup_{Y \neq 0} \frac{\lVert J_g^{\mathfrak{se}(3)} Y \rVert_{\mathbb{R}^d}}{\lVert Y \rVert_{\mathbf{W}}}.

By the change of variables u = \mathbf{W}^{1/2} Y, we obtain

\lVert D e_P(g) \rVert_{\text{op}, \mathrm{SE}(3) \to \mathbb{R}} = \lVert J_g^{\mathfrak{se}(3)} \mathbf{W}^{-1/2} \rVert_{\text{op}} \le \lVert J_g^{\mathfrak{se}(3)} \rVert_{\text{op}} \cdot \lVert \mathbf{W}^{-1/2} \rVert_{\text{op}} = \frac{\lVert J_g^{\mathfrak{se}(3)} \rVert_{\text{op}}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{W})}},

where \sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{W}) > 0 is the smallest eigenvalue of the mass‑inertia matrix. Hence the global Lipschitz constant K_P satisfies

K_P \le \frac{\sup_{g} \lVert J_g^{\mathfrak{se}(3)} \rVert_{\text{op}}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{W})}}.

This bound is finite as long as all objects have strictly positive mass and rotational inertia.

