Appendix C: Causal Geometry — Differentiable Distance, Identifiability, and Finsler Extension

C.1 Softmin All‑Paths Kernel

To achieve a fully differentiable shortest‑path distance matrix D_C \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d} over a learned DAG, we define a smooth sum‑over‑paths partition function.

Let A \in [0,1]^{d \times d} be the continuous adjacency matrix, and W \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^{d \times d} be a non‑negative edge length matrix. For a temperature parameter \tau > 0, define the softmin transition matrix

\tilde{M}_{ij} = A_{ij} \exp\left(-\frac{W_{ij}}{\tau}\right).

Because A is constrained to be acyclic (via NOTEARS), \tilde{M} is nilpotent, i.e., \tilde{M}^d = 0. The sum over all directed paths of any length is then

Z = \sum_{k=1}^{d-1} \tilde{M}^k = (I - \tilde{M})^{-1} - I.

The soft shortest‑path distance is extracted via the log‑sum‑exp:

D_C(\tau)_{ij} = -\tau \log\left( Z_{ij} + \epsilon \right),

where \epsilon > 0 prevents numerical underflow for disconnected nodes and sets a maximum distance bound d_{\max} = -\tau \log \epsilon.

Proposition 3 (Convergence to true shortest path).
As \tau \to 0^+, D_C(\tau)_{ij} converges to the geodesic shortest path length if a directed path exists, and to d_{\max} otherwise.

Proof.
As \tau \to 0, the exponential weighting concentrates all mass on paths with minimal total length, and the softmin converges to the minimum. The nilpotency ensures the series is finite and exact. ∎

Proposition 4 (Differentiability).
D_C is differentiable with respect to both A and W provided (I - \tilde{M}) is invertible.

Proof.
The map \tilde{M} \mapsto (I - \tilde{M})^{-1} is smooth on the open set of invertible matrices. Since \tilde{M} is a smooth function of A and W, and the logarithm is smooth for positive inputs, the composition is differentiable. ∎

The gradient with respect to \tilde{M} is given by the standard matrix differential:

\frac{\partial Z}{\partial \tilde{M}} = (I - \tilde{M})^{-T} \otimes (I - \tilde{M})^{-1},

ensuring non‑zero gradients even for non‑adjacent nodes.

C.2 Identifiability Theorem

We rely on the following result from causal representation learning (Lee et al.):

Theorem 5 (Identifiability of latent causal models).
Consider a latent linear Gaussian structural causal model with d variables. Suppose we have data from m environments, each corresponding to a stochastic hard intervention on a subset of variables. If m = \mathcal{O}(\log d) and each node is intervened upon at least twice, then the latent causal graph, the latent representations, and the decoder are identifiable up to permutation and elementwise scaling.

In our framework, the latent variables are physical properties (mass, charge, initial velocity), and interventions correspond to setting these to fixed values in a simulator. This theorem guarantees that, in principle, the causal graph can be recovered from interventional data. For nonlinear dynamics, we use a nonlinear structural equation model (e.g., a normalizing flow), and theoretical guarantees extend under appropriate assumptions (e.g., nonlinear ICA with interventions).

C.3 Finsler Causal Geometry

To rigorously model asymmetric reachability, the causal geometry G_C is extended from a Riemannian manifold (\mathcal{M}_C, g_C) to a Finsler manifold (\mathcal{M}_C, F_C), where F_C: T\mathcal{M}_C \to [0, \infty) is a continuous, asymmetric Minkowski norm defined on the tangent bundle.

Definition C.1 (Finsler Causal Structure).
A Finsler structure F_C(x, v) on the smooth manifold \mathcal{M}_C satisfies:

· Smoothness: F_C(x, v) is C^\infty on T\mathcal{M}_C \setminus \{0\}.
· Positive Homogeneity: F_C(x, \lambda v) = \lambda F_C(x, v) for all \lambda > 0.
· Strong Convexity: The fundamental tensor g_{ij}(x, v) = \frac{1}{2} \frac{\partial^2 F_C^2(x, v)}{\partial v^i \partial v^j} is positive definite for all v \neq 0.
· Directional Asymmetry (Non‑Reversibility): In general, F_C(x, -v) \neq F_C(x, v).

C.3.1 Causal Randers Structure & Drift Mechanics

Definition C.2 (Causal Randers Metric).
The Finsler structure F_C(x, v) is parameterized as:

F_C(x, v) = \alpha(x, v) + \beta(x, v) = \sqrt{a_{ij}(x) v^i v^j} + b_i(x) v^i,

where:

· a_{ij}(x) dx^i dx^j is a symmetric Riemannian metric encoding ambient local graph density.
· b(x) = b_i(x) dx^i is a learned 1‑form representing the causal drift field.

To guarantee strong convexity, the anisotropic drift norm is constrained such that \lVert b \rVert_a = \sqrt{a^{ij}(x) b_i(x) b_j(x)} < 1.

Interpretation of Causal Drift.
The vector b^i(x) = a^{ij}(x) b_j(x) points in the direction of downstream causal flow. Moving along the causal arrow (v aligned with b) decreases the tangent cost F_C(x, v), whereas moving against the causal arrow (v anti‑aligned with b) increases F_C(x, v):

\begin{array}{ccc}
\text{Downstream } (v \sim +b) & & \text{Upstream } (-v \sim -b) \\
F_C(x,v) = \lVert v \rVert_a + \langle b, v \rangle & \qquad & F_C(x,-v) = \lVert v \rVert_a - \langle b, v \rangle \\
\text{Low asymmetric distance} & & \text{High asymmetric distance}
\end{array}

C.3.2 Asymmetric Geodesic Distance and Causal Cones

Given a piecewise smooth path \gamma: [0, 1] \to \mathcal{M}_C with \gamma(0) = p and \gamma(1) = q, the asymmetric Finsler arc length is:

L_{F_C}(\gamma) = \int_0^1 F_C\left(\gamma(t), \dot{\gamma}(t)\right) dt.

Definition C.3 (Finsler Quasi‑Metric).
The causal asymmetric distance d_F(p, q) from point p to point q is defined as:

d_F(p, q) = \inf_{\gamma \in \mathcal{P}(p, q)} L_{F_C}(\gamma),

where \mathcal{P}(p, q) is the set of all oriented paths from p to q. Crucially:

d_F(p, q) \neq d_F(q, p) \quad \text{for } p \neq q.

Causal Cones.
The forward causal cone C_p^+ at p \in \mathcal{M}_C for a maximum reachability threshold R > 0 is defined by:

C_p^+ = \{ q \in \mathcal{M}_C : d_F(p, q) \le R \}.

Because F_C(x, v) < F_C(x, -v) along the drift b, C_p^+ stretches preferentially downstream along the directed edges of the learned DAG.

C.3.3 Continuous Graph Mapping & Differentiable Eikonal Solver

To bridge the Finsler metric F_C(x, v) with the discrete adjacency matrix A and edge lengths W, we construct a_{ij}(x) and b_i(x) via node embeddings z_i \in \mathcal{M}_C.

Metric Formulation:

a_{ij}(x) = \delta_{ij} \cdot \psi_\theta(x),

where \psi_\theta(x) > 0 is a scalar field parameterized by a multi‑layer perceptron.

Causal Drift Field Formulation:

b(x) = \tanh\left( \text{MLP}_\phi(x) \right) \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \epsilon},

ensuring \lVert b(x) \rVert_a < 1 globally.

Asymmetric Distance Computation via Non‑symmetric Eikonal Equation:
The forward distance map u(x) = d_F(p, x) from source point p is the unique viscosity solution to the dual Finsler–Eikonal equation:

F_C^*\left(x, d_x u\right) = 1, \quad u(p) = 0,

where F_C^*(x, y) is the dual Finsler metric on T^*\mathcal{M}_C:

F_C^*(x, y) = \sup \{ y_i v^i : v \in T_x \mathcal{M}_C, \, F_C(x, v) \le 1 \}.

For the Randers metric, the dual norm simplifies to:

F_C^*(x, y) = \frac{\sqrt{\left(a^{ij} y_i y_j\right)\left(1 - \lVert b \rVert_a^2\right) + \left(a^{ij} y_i b_j\right)^2} - a^{ij} y_i b_j}{1 - \lVert b \rVert_a^2}.

This Hamilton–Jacobi formulation allows for differentiable, neural‑network‑compatible solvers (e.g., Anisotropic Fast Marching or Neural PDE Solvers) to compute d_F(p, q) across all latent nodes in a single forward pass.

C.3.4 Theoretical Properties of the Finsler Causal Distance

Proposition 7 (Asymmetry Bound under Randers Drift).
Let \gamma_{p \to q} be the minimizing forward geodesic from p to q under F_C, and \bar{\gamma}_{q \to p} be the time‑reversed curve. The degree of directional asymmetry is bounded by the circulation of the drift 1‑form b:

d_F(q, p) - d_F(p, q) \ge -2 \int_{\gamma_{p \to q}} b_i(x) dx^i.

Proof.
By definition of the Randers metric:

d_F(p, q) = \int_0^1 \left( \alpha(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) + \beta(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) \right) dt.

For the reversed path \bar{\gamma}(t) = \gamma(1-t), we have \dot{\bar{\gamma}}(t) = -\dot{\gamma}(1-t). Since \alpha(x, -v) = \alpha(x, v) and \beta(x, -v) = -\beta(x, v):

L_{F_C}(\bar{\gamma}) = \int_0^1 \left( \alpha(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) - \beta(\gamma, \dot{\gamma}) \right) dt.

Because d_F(q, p) \le L_{F_C}(\bar{\gamma}), taking the difference gives:

d_F(q, p) - d_F(p, q) \ge \int_0^1 \left( \alpha - \beta \right) dt - \int_0^1 \left( \alpha + \beta \right) dt = -2 \int_0^1 b_i(\gamma(t)) \dot{\gamma}^i(t) dt = -2 \int_{\gamma_{p \to q}} b_i(x) dx^i.

∎

Theorem 8 (Quasi‑Metric Properties of d_F).
The asymmetric distance function d_F: \mathcal{M}_C \times \mathcal{M}_C \to [0, \infty) satisfies all quasi‑metric axioms:

1. Positivity: d_F(p, q) \ge 0, and d_F(p, q) = 0 \iff p = q.
2. Directed Triangle Inequality: For any p, q, r \in \mathcal{M}_C:

d_F(p, r) \le d_F(p, q) + d_F(q, r).

Proof.
Positivity follows directly from strong convexity and positive definiteness of F_C(x, v) for v \neq 0 since \lVert b \rVert_a < 1.

Let \gamma_1: [0, 1] \to \mathcal{M}_C be the minimizing geodesic from p to q, and \gamma_2: [0, 1] \to \mathcal{M}_C from q to r. Concatenate \gamma = \gamma_1 * \gamma_2 on [0, 2]. By reparameterization invariance (homogeneity of degree 1):

d_F(p, r) \le L_{F_C}(\gamma) = L_{F_C}(\gamma_1) + L_{F_C}(\gamma_2) = d_F(p, q) + d_F(q, r).

∎

