Appendix D: Physical Geometry — Symplectic Structure, Energy Conservation, and Coupled Hamiltonian Dynamics

D.1 Configuration Manifold and Lie Group

For K rigid bodies, the configuration manifold is \mathcal{Q} = \mathrm{SE}(3)^K. An element g_k \in \mathrm{SE}(3) represents the pose (position and orientation) of object k, written in homogeneous coordinates as

g_k = \begin{pmatrix} R_k & p_k \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \quad R_k \in \mathrm{SO}(3), \; p_k \in \mathbb{R}^3.

The tangent space at the identity is the Lie algebra \mathfrak{se}(3), consisting of twists \xi = (v, \omega)^T \in \mathbb{R}^6, where v is linear velocity and \omega angular velocity.

D.2 Phase Space and Symplectic Form

The phase space is the cotangent bundle \mathcal{M}_P = T^*\mathcal{Q}. In left‑trivialized coordinates, a state is (q,p) with q \in \mathcal{Q} and p \in \mathfrak{se}(3)^*. The canonical symplectic form on T^*\mathcal{Q} is not globally \sum_i dq^i \wedge dp_i for non‑Euclidean \mathcal{Q}. Instead, it includes a curvature term involving the structure constants c_{ijk} of \mathfrak{se}(3):

\omega = \sum_i dq^i \wedge dp_i + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j,k} c_{ijk} p_k \, dq^i \wedge dq^j.

In local coordinates, the structure constants are fixed by the Lie bracket of \mathfrak{se}(3).

D.3 Hamiltonian Dynamics and Energy Conservation

The Hamiltonian is

H(q,p) = \frac{1}{2} p^T \mathbf{W}^{-1} p + V(q),

where \mathbf{W} is the mass‑inertia matrix and V(q) the potential energy. The Hamiltonian vector field X_H is defined by \iota_{X_H} \omega = dH. In the Lie–Poisson formulation, the equations of motion are

\dot{q} = \frac{\partial H}{\partial p}, \quad \dot{p} = - \frac{\partial H}{\partial q} - \operatorname{ad}^*_{\frac{\partial H}{\partial p}} p,

where \operatorname{ad}^* is the coadjoint action of \mathfrak{se}(3) on its dual. This extra term arises from the curvature of the group and is essential for energy conservation.

Theorem 4 (Energy conservation).
Along trajectories of X_H, the Hamiltonian is constant: dH/dt = 0.

Proof.
Using the Poisson bracket induced by \omega, we have \frac{dH}{dt} = \{H, H\} = 0, which holds for any antisymmetric bracket. Hence H is conserved. ∎

D.4 Geodesic Distance on \mathrm{SE}(3)

The left‑invariant Riemannian metric on \mathrm{SE}(3) is determined by \mathbf{W}. The geodesic distance between two poses g_1, g_2 \in \mathrm{SE}(3) is

d_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}(g_1, g_2) = \left\lVert \log_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}(g_1^{-1} g_2) \right\rVert_{\mathbf{W}},

where \log_{\mathrm{SE}(3)}: \mathrm{SE}(3) \to \mathfrak{se}(3) is the logarithmic map, and the norm is the weighted Euclidean norm in the Lie algebra.

D.5 Coupled Physical–Core Hamiltonian Dynamics

To couple the physical phase space \mathcal{M}_P = T^*\mathrm{SE}(3)^K \cong \mathrm{SE}(3)^K \times (\mathfrak{se}(3)^*)^K with the d_{\text{core}}-dimensional dynamical core \mathcal{W}_{\text{dyn}}, we formalize the extended product manifold:

\mathcal{M}_{\text{ext}} = \mathcal{M}_P \times \mathcal{W}_{\text{dyn}}.

A state in \mathcal{M}_{\text{ext}} is represented as (q, \mu, z), where:

· q = (g_1, \dots, g_K) \in \mathrm{SE}(3)^K represents body configuration poses.
· \mu = (\mu_1, \dots, \mu_K) \in (\mathfrak{se}(3)^*)^K represents body/left‑reduced momenta.
· z \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{core}}} represents the core latent vector, equipped with a smooth skew‑symmetric Poisson structure tensor J(z) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{core}} \times d_{\text{core}}} (where J^T(z) = -J(z)).

D.5.1 Total Poisson Bracket

For smooth observables F, G \in C^\infty(\mathcal{M}_{\text{ext}}), the total Poisson bracket decomposes into physical, core, and interaction components:

\{F, G\}_{\text{ext}}(q, \mu, z) = \{F, G\}_P(q, \mu) + \{F, G\}_{\text{core}}(z).

Left‑Trivialized Physical Bracket on T^*\mathrm{SE}(3)^K:

\{F, G\}_P(q, \mu) = \sum_{k=1}^K \left( \left\langle \frac{\partial F}{\partial \mu_k}, L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial G}{\partial g_k} \right\rangle - \left\langle \frac{\partial G}{\partial \mu_k}, L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial F}{\partial g_k} \right\rangle - \left\langle \mu_k, \left[ \frac{\partial F}{\partial \mu_k}, \frac{\partial G}{\partial \mu_k} \right] \right\rangle \right),

where [\cdot, \cdot] is the Lie bracket on \mathfrak{se}(3), \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle denotes the pairing between \mathfrak{se}(3)^* and \mathfrak{se}(3), and L_g^* is the dual of left‑translation differential.

Core Poisson Bracket:

\{F, G\}_{\text{core}}(z) = \left( \nabla_z F \right)^T J(z) \left( \nabla_z G \right).

D.5.2 Coupled Total Hamiltonian

The evolution of the coupled system is governed by a total Hamiltonian H_{\text{total}} \in C^\infty(\mathcal{M}_{\text{ext}}):

H_{\text{total}}(q, \mu, z) = H_P(q, \mu) + H_{\text{core}}(z) + H_{\text{int}}(q, \mu, z),

where:

· Uncoupled Physical Energy: H_P(q, \mu) = \sum_{k=1}^K \frac{1}{2} \left\langle \mu_k, \mathbf{W}_k^{-1} \mu_k \right\rangle + V(q), with inertia tensor \mathbf{W}_k \succ 0 and potential energy V(q).
· Uncoupled Core Energy: H_{\text{core}}(z) defines internal latent dynamics.
· Bi‑directional Coupling Potential: Mediated by the differentiable projection encoder e_P: \mathcal{M}_P \to \mathcal{W}_{\text{dyn}}:

H_{\text{int}}(q, \mu, z) = \frac{\lambda}{2} \left\lVert e_P(q, \mu) - z \right\rVert_2^2,

where \lambda > 0 controls the coupling rigidity.

D.5.3 Explicit Lie–Poisson Motion Equations

The temporal evolution of any function A(q, \mu, z) follows Hamilton’s equation \dot{A} = \{A, H_{\text{total}}\}_{\text{ext}}. Evaluating coordinate projections yields:

1. Configuration Kinematics on \mathrm{SE}(3).
For each object k \in \{1, \dots, K\}:

\dot{g}_k = L_{g_k} \left( \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k} \right).

Expanding \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k}:

\frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k} = \mathbf{W}_k^{-1} \mu_k + \lambda \left( D_{\mu_k} e_P(q, \mu) \right)^* \left( e_P(q, \mu) - z \right).

Let \xi_k^{\text{eff}} = \mathbf{W}_k^{-1} \mu_k + \lambda \left( D_{\mu_k} e_P \right)^* (e_P - z) \in \mathfrak{se}(3) be the effective body velocity twist. The kinematic equation becomes:

\dot{g}_k = g_k \cdot \left( \xi_k^{\text{eff}} \right)^\wedge,

where (\cdot)^\wedge : \mathfrak{se}(3) \to \mathfrak{se}(3) \subset \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4} is the standard Lie algebra matrix mapping.

2. Momentum Dynamics on Coadjoint Space \mathfrak{se}(3)^*.
Using the coadjoint operator \operatorname{ad}^*:

\dot{\mu}_k = \operatorname{ad}^*_{\frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k}} \mu_k - L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial g_k}.

Expanding \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial g_k}:

\frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial g_k} = \frac{\partial V}{\partial g_k} + \lambda \left( D_{g_k} e_P(q, \mu) \right)^* \left( e_P(q, \mu) - z \right).

Substituting yields the Lie–Poisson momentum equation with core reaction forces:

\dot{\mu}_k = \operatorname{ad}^*_{\xi_k^{\text{eff}}} \mu_k - L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial V}{\partial g_k} - \lambda \underbrace{L_{g_k}^* \left( D_{g_k} e_P \right)^* \left( e_P(q, \mu) - z \right)}_{\tau_k^{\text{core}} \text{ (Core Feedback Wrench)}}.

In matrix form using structure constants c_{ij}^m of \mathfrak{se}(3):

\dot{\mu}_{k, m} = \sum_{i,j} c_{ij}^m \mu_{k, j} \xi_{k, i}^{\text{eff}} - \left( L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial V}{\partial g_k} \right)_m - \lambda \left( L_{g_k}^* (D_{g_k} e_P)^* (e_P - z) \right)_m.

3. Core Latent Dynamics.
For the core vector z \in \mathcal{W}_{\text{dyn}}:

\dot{z} = J(z) \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} = J(z) \left( \nabla_z H_{\text{core}}(z) - \lambda \left( e_P(q, \mu) - z \right) \right).

D.5.4 Conservation Laws and Geometric Exactness

Theorem 9 (Energy Conservation of Coupled System).
Along trajectories of the coupled Lie–Poisson flow \dot{x} = X_{H_{\text{total}}}(x) on \mathcal{M}_{\text{ext}}, the total energy H_{\text{total}} is strictly conserved:

\frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}}(q(t), \mu(t), z(t)) = 0.

Proof.
By the chain rule and skew‑symmetry of the extended Poisson bracket \{F, G\}_{\text{ext}} = -\{G, F\}_{\text{ext}}:

\frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}} = \{H_{\text{total}}, H_{\text{total}}\}_{\text{ext}} = \{H_{\text{total}}, H_{\text{total}}\}_P + \{H_{\text{total}}, H_{\text{total}}\}_{\text{core}}.

For the physical Poisson bracket:

\{H_{\text{total}}, H_{\text{total}}\}_P = \sum_{k=1}^K \left( \left\langle \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k}, L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial g_k} \right\rangle - \left\langle \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k}, L_{g_k}^* \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial g_k} \right\rangle - \left\langle \mu_k, \left[ \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k}, \frac{\partial H_{\text{total}}}{\partial \mu_k} \right] \right\rangle \right) = 0,

due to symmetry cancellation of pairing terms and skew‑symmetry of the Lie bracket [\xi, \xi] = 0.

For the core Poisson bracket:

\{H_{\text{total}}, H_{\text{total}}\}_{\text{core}} = \left( \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} \right)^T J(z) \left( \nabla_z H_{\text{total}} \right) = 0,

due to skew‑symmetry J(z) = -J^T(z).

Therefore, \frac{d}{dt} H_{\text{total}} = 0 + 0 = 0. ∎

