Appendix F: VICReg Regularization and Collapse Prevention

For a batch Z \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d}, the VICReg regularization consists of:

1. Variance loss:
   \mathcal{L}_{\text{var}}(Z) = \frac{1}{d} \sum_{k=1}^{d} \max\left(0, \gamma - \sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(Z[:,k])}\right),
   where \gamma > 0 is a target standard deviation.
2. Covariance loss:
   \mathcal{L}_{\text{cov}}(Z) = \frac{1}{d^2} \sum_{k \neq l} \operatorname{Cov}(Z[:,k], Z[:,l])^2.

The total regularization applied to each geometry and the core is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}} = \sum_{i \in \{\text{geometries}, \text{core}\}} \left( \mathcal{L}_{\text{var}}^{(i)} + \mathcal{L}_{\text{cov}}^{(i)} \right).

Theorem 3 (Collapse prevention).
Assume encoder outputs are bounded: \lVert z_i \rVert \le M for all i. If \mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}} = 0, then every feature has standard deviation at least \gamma, and features are pairwise uncorrelated. In particular, the representation cannot collapse to a single point.

Proof.
If \mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}} = 0, each variance loss term must be zero, implying \sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(Z[:,k])} \ge \gamma for all k. If the representation collapsed to a constant vector, all variances would be zero, contradicting the bound. Similarly, each covariance term must be zero, so \operatorname{Cov}(Z[:,k], Z[:,l]) = 0 for all k \neq l. ∎

