Appendix H: Complexity Analysis

· Star‑topology alignment: \mathcal{O}(K N^2) vs. all‑pairs \mathcal{O}(K^2 N^2).
· Differentiable causal distance (softmin): \mathcal{O}(d^3) per batch due to matrix inversion; for small d (number of latent variables), this is negligible.
· Finsler Eikonal solver: Complexity depends on the numerical method (e.g., Anisotropic Fast Marching is \mathcal{O}(N \log N) per node), offering potential scalability over matrix inversion for large graphs.
· Local neighborhood preservation: \mathcal{O}(K N \log N) with efficient kNN approximations.
· Coupled Hamiltonian integration: Requires symplectic integrators (e.g., Lie–Poisson integrators) whose cost scales with the dimension of the phase space and core, typically \mathcal{O}(K \cdot \dim(\mathfrak{se}(3)) + d_{\text{core}}) per step.

